**Requirements.** Create files and directories, nest directories, `list`, `delete`, get size and path; directories contain files and directories; later add features (permissions, compression, logging) without rewriting the core.

**Entities (composite pattern).**

```java
abstract class Item {
    protected String name; protected Directory parent;
    abstract long getSize();
    abstract void list(int depth);
    void delete() { if (parent != null) parent.remove(this); }
}
class File extends Item {
    private byte[] content;
    long getSize() { return content.length; }
    void list(int d) { /* print name */ }
}
class Directory extends Item {
    private Map<String, Item> children = new HashMap<>();
    void add(Item i) { children.put(i.name, i); i.parent = this; }
    void remove(Item i) { children.remove(i.name); }
    long getSize() { return children.values().stream().mapToLong(Item::getSize).sum(); }
    void list(int d) { children.values().forEach(c -> c.list(d + 1)); }
}
```
Clients treat `File` and `Directory` uniformly through `Item`, and `list` and `delete` recurse naturally. The structure is a **tree**: a root `Directory` holds children. Use a map for O(1) lookup by name, and resolve paths by splitting on `/`.

**Dynamic functionality.** Prefer **composition** to deep inheritance:
- Give items optional components or strategies (a `PermissionPolicy`, `Compressor`, `Logger`) injected at construction or runtime.
- Or wrap with the **decorator pattern** (`LoggedItem`, `EncryptedFile`) that implements `Item` and delegates.
- Use interfaces to define contracts between parts.

**Edge cases and trade-offs.** Name clashes, deleting non-empty directories (recursive delete), cycles (disallow moving a directory into its own descendant), thread safety on shared trees, and sizes cached versus computed.

**Follow-up: database indexing and B-trees.** An index is a separate sorted structure mapping column values to row locations, turning a full scan O(n) into a lookup O(log n). A **B-tree (B+ tree)** is a balanced, wide, multi-way tree whose nodes match disk pages, so a lookup needs few disk reads (height is typically 3 to 4). B+ trees keep all keys in linked leaves, supporting range queries well. Cost: extra storage and slower writes, since indexes must be updated.
